WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.970
- This is a period of great transition.

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.890
It's a seismic shift effort.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.510
- Maybe the biggest revolution

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.000
since we moved from the
horse to the engine.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.100
- [Narrator] If I say
electric vehicle today,

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.940
many people think of one name,

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.150
but many startups want to change that,

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.250
Rivian, Lordstown, Fisker, Lucid and Canoo

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:20.550
are all in a race to release new vehicles.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.040
- And it's gonna get to
be a pretty crowded space.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.880
- [Narrator] In 2020, 4
out of 5 EVs sold in the US

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.840
were Teslas, most of the others were made

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.610
by traditional automakers.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.800
As analysts project that the
market for EVs will grow 140%

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.840
by 2026, new contenders
are pitching themselves

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.750
as the next big thing.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.040
And they have one thing in common.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.750
- We haven't sold a single car.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.050
- [Narrator] Even though
they haven't produced

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:43.580
market ready cars yet,

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.120
they've secured millions,
sometimes billions of dollars

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.720
in funding, but their
strategies are very different

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.220
and car analysts say not
all of them will survive.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.010
- That's probably a little too
many EV startups right now.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.930
- There's a whole bunch of other wannabes

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:59.052
out there that exists because of Tesla

00:00:59.052 --> 00:01:02.070
who have no concept that
this is a technology race.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.260
- [Narrator] So how are these companies

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.990
trying to win the great EV race.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.883
Investors have collectively
pumped over $10 billion

00:01:08.883 --> 00:01:10.390
into these companies,

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:14.030
with Rivian receiving the
most, some $8.2 billion

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:17.190
of investment coming from
Ford, Amazon, and BlackRock.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:18.630
We asked to speak to Rivian's CEO,

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.130
but the company said they wanted to wait

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.870
until they'd started production.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:25.420
Lucid is second with some $7.4 billion.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.344
- We're now in a very
secure financial position

00:01:28.344 --> 00:01:31.590
and it's up to management
not to screw this up.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.020
- [Narrator] That's Peter
Rawlinson, Lucid CEO.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.550
The company recently
received more than $2 billion

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.810
through Saudi Arabia's
Public Investment Fund

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.530
and also received $4.6
billion through a merger

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.330
with a special purpose
acquisition company or SPAC.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.450
SPACs are essentially
empty shell companies

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.170
set up with the sole purpose

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.440
of raising money to merge
with another business

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.100
and then take that business public.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.950
Fisker too has seen
some $1 billion injected

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:56.830
into the company through its SPAC merger

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.710
with Apollo Global Management,

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.902
Henrik Fisker is Fisker's CEO.

00:02:00.902 --> 00:02:01.735
- I think that's great news

00:02:01.735 --> 00:02:04.810
for everybody for us and for
customers that are looking

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:07.630
for new type of vehicles,
new technologies et cetera.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:08.630
- [Narrator] Lordstown and Canoo

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:12.780
have both received less
funding, under $1 billion each.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.640
But those investors
are betting on vehicles

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.720
targeted at very different customers,

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.500
ones that aren't
necessarily going to compete

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:19.710
against each other.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.100
Lucid is trying to capture
the top end of the market

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:27.100
with its high-end luxury
$169,000 Lucid Air Dream Edition.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.210
It's CEO knows a thing
or two about sedans.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.690
- I led the design of
engineering of Model S,

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.870
Tesla's premium it's not true luxury.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.930
We see a huge space in
the luxury EV space.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.551
- They might have some more challenges

00:02:40.551 --> 00:02:44.820
because you look at
those $150,000 vehicles,

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.550
and you know, they're pretty established.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.750
- [Narrator] But at the other
end of the market is Fisker.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.590
They're making an SUV called the Ocean

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:55.270
which will sell for
between 35 and $70,000.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.490
It's made partly with recycled material.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.680
- I think most EV startups start

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.110
with a sports car luxury car,

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.339
cause they kind of need to prove

00:03:02.339 --> 00:03:04.650
that they can make a cool car,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.740
so they feel that this is
necessary for them to do.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:08.320
I don't think we need to prove that.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.960
I truly think the next
frontier is affordability,

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.640
value for money and getting high volume

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.500
into the market of EVs.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.870
- [Narrator] Lordstown, Canoo
and Rivian are also targeting

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:21.450
the utility vehicle segment,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:25.050
by developing pickup
trucks and this microbus.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.600
- If you want to have a
bit of a chance really,

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.050
you kinda need to get into
that utility vehicle segment

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.750
because that's what consumers are buying.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:35.250
However, everybody's getting
into the utility vehicle

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.040
segment because that's
what consumers are buying.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.090
- [Narrator] Before selling
these vehicles to consumers,

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.010
these startups first need to make them.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.010
And some of them say they've been held

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:47.210
up by the Corona virus pandemic
and supply chain issues.

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:48.690
- They've missed a lot of their goals.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.200
They've missed a lot of their targets.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.870
They do jump in thinking
it's going to be a little bit

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:55.430
easier than it is or that
they can move it faster

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:58.440
than they can, and they
get into it and realize

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.600
that there are just
elements you can't rush.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.550
- [Narrator] When Tesla began
production of their Model S,

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:06.240
they decided to move almost
all parts of manufacturing

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.260
in house and Lucid is
doing exactly the same.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.300
- Tesla's in a preeminent position

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.640
because it has hardcore
technology in house.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.247
That is why it's worth what

00:04:16.247 --> 00:04:19.585
$650 billion and other companies aren't.

00:04:19.585 --> 00:04:20.980
- [Narrator] Rivian
and Lordstown meanwhile

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.390
have acquired former automotive
manufacturing facilities,

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:26.170
but companies like Canoo and Fisker

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.280
are instead outsourcing
their manufacturing

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.370
to other companies.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.440
- Most companies don't get it.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.447
They think doing an EV is
you just buy all these parts

00:04:34.447 --> 00:04:37.900
off the shelf and inclusion
together in a Frankenstein,

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.750
and that's a battery EV.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:43.100
- We are less interested
in sort of this ego trip

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.420
of having to show everybody else

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:46.030
that you can manufacture a car.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.790
Normally the car industry
does is they bring a new car

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:53.760
into an existing factory
with very trained personnel

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.760
to avoid any hiccups.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.170
And that's essentially what we're doing.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:04:59.540
- [Narrator] When these
vehicles do eventually

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.870
come to market, you'll be
hard pressed to buy one

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:03.840
in a traditional car dealership.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.452
Like Tesla, most of these startups

00:05:05.452 --> 00:05:07.770
plan on selling direct to the consumer,

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.640
but Fisker and Canoo said
they would also offer

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.440
subscription models much
like some other EV companies

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:13.570
do in China.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.260
- You see a lot of people, specifically,

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.961
a lot of younger people are
really interested in this,

00:05:17.961 --> 00:05:18.794
because a lot of young people

00:05:18.794 --> 00:05:22.450
really don't wanna go and
borrow $50,000 or euros.

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:23.610
- [Narrator] Lordstown on the other hand

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:27.590
is hoping to target fleet
operators with its pickup truck.

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:31.150
These vehicles won't only
be competing against Tesla.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:34.110
There's also many startups
in countries like China,

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.580
and of course, there's
the legacy car industry.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.570
- They are also competing
against the traditional

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:39.950
automakers, and let me tell you,

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.540
they're woken up and they're serious,

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:43.800
and they're not messing around,

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.763
and a lot of the things that
were considered disruptors

00:05:45.763 --> 00:05:48.890
are very rapidly filtering
through the industry

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:50.970
for any kind of vehicle you wanna buy,

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:53.010
which means the startups

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:56.320
don't necessarily have
that unique proposition.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.720
- [Narrator] General Motors
said it's investing $27 billion

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.890
in EVs and autonomous driving.

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:04.160
It pans to make 30
electric models by 2025,

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.940
and is spending $2.2
billion to convert a factory

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:09.410
in Detroit into its new EV hub.

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:11.850
- This is a change in trajectory,

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.850
for society and for General Motors

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.330
- [Narrator] Other car
makers are actually betting

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:18.050
on the startups succeeding.

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:21.170
Ford, for example has
invested some $500 million

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:24.410
into Rivian, but Fisker
and Lucid are both betting

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:25.590
that they can replace some

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.100
of those automakers
within the next decade.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.640
- I think we're gonna say goodbye

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.890
to a few of the traditional car companies

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.050
that just won't even be there in 10 years.

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:36.287
And then I think you're gonna see some

00:06:36.287 --> 00:06:37.970
of those traditional car companies

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:42.490
may be sort of shrink and become smaller.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:44.610
- That change of the order of the guard

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.653
has already commenced,

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:49.660
but there's going to be a continuation

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.550
of that over the next decade.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.732
I think that there's gonna
be blood on the carpet.

00:06:54.732 --> 00:06:57.149
(calm music)

